{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The tin stayed on the windowsill all winter. Frost feathered the glass in January, and some mornings the girl pressed her palm against the pane until a clear circle opened and she could see the bare garden below, the canes still standing in their wigwam like the bones of something that had gone to sleep. In February she took the lid off once, just to look. The beans had not changed, but she felt somehow that they were listening. By March the light came earlier, thin and lemon-coloured, and her mother bought a bag of compost from the garden centre and set it by the back door. They planted the new seeds first, one to each yoghurt pot, pushing them down with a fingertip to the depth of a knuckle. Her brother wrote the names on lolly sticks in wobbly capitals and spelled Painted wrong, and nobody corrected him. Then the girl took out the envelope from 1998. Her mother said the old beans might be too old to wake, that seeds could only wait so long. The girl planted all six anyway, in a pot of their own, and set it at the very front of the windowsill where the sun would find it first. Every morning before school she checked. The new beans came up in nine days, hooked and pale, shouldering the soil aside. The old pot stayed dark and still. On the nineteenth day, when she had almost stopped hoping, a single green loop broke the surface, bent like a question, and slowly began to straighten."}
